            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In biblical theology, particularly in the New Testament, 'life' or 'the life' primarily symbolizes eternal, divine life imparted to believers, with Christ Himself often seen as the very embodiment and source of this life (e.g., John 14:6).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines 'life' or 'the life' as covenant existence—specifically, a life lived in obedience to God's law.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Eternal/divine life or the person of Christ.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Covenant existence and obedience to God's law.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book applies an Old Testament covenantal understanding of 'life' to all instances, including New Testament christological titles (like John 14:6), completely replacing the consensus view of an ontological divine gift or person with a behavioral state.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus identifies 'the life' as an imparted divine nature or Christ Himself; the book identifies it as the human action and state of walking according to God's law.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book explicitly contrasts its definition with the common view ('Christ Himself, and the eternal life imparted to believers'). By defining 'the life' as covenant obedience, it materially changes the referent from an ontological and christological reality to a behavioral and covenantal one.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"John 14:6 ('I am the way, the truth, and the life') presents Jesus not merely as a teacher or exemplar of covenant obedience, but as the literal embodiment and source of divine life itself.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
